-------Data Types-------
    An attribute of data which tells the compiler/interpreter how the programmer intends to use the data. Data Types define the operations that can be done on the data, the meaning of the data, and the way values of that type can be stored.
    1. Strings: "Hello World!"
    2. Numbers/Integers: 0, -12, 200
    3. Floats: 0.00, -12.00, 200.00
    4. Booleans: true, false
    5. Null: null

-------Literal Value-------
    A literal is a value that's written directly in the source code as opposed to being the result of some other expression (such as referencing a variable or a constant).

-------Variable/Scalar-------
    Is a storage address paired with an associated symbolic name, which contains a known/unknown quantity of information, often referred to as a value. You should use variables when you have that needs to change over the lifetime of the game/program.

    Format for Declaring Variables:
    var uniqueName (:Data Type) (= Literal/Data Object)

    Examples:
    var aString: String = 'hi friend'
    var aNumber: int = 10
    var aFloat: float = -12.0
    var aBoolean: boolean = true

    Or:
    var aString := 'hi friend'
    var aNumber := 10
    var aFloat := -12.0
    var aBoolean := true

-------Memory Life Cycle-------
    No matter the programming language, memory life cycle is the same. Memory is allocated by the operating system which allows your program to use it. All computers have memory, also known as RAM (Random Access Memory). Memory can be thought of aas an array of bytes.

Adress | Value
 0x142 |
 0x144 |
  ...  |
 0x148 |

Use Memory
    Read/write operations are happening as you are using the allocated memory, assigning and changing variable values in your script/code.

Release Memory
    When variable(s) are no longer in use, release the entire memory so that it is freed and can be used to allocate new memory. It depends on the programming language you are using whether this is done manually or automatically.

General Tip for Memory
    The less memory you use, the better it is in general. For games, using less memory takes into consideratiion the performance for users who are using lower size rams in computers.

Going over Allocated Memory
    If there is enough space, the operating system will allocate more memory from RAM to your application/game. If you go over the RAM limit, the operating system will start allocating memory to the Hard Drive/Solid State. Using the HD/SSD for memory is slower than using RAM.

