GDScript Tip for Memory Management
    Nodes are not refenced counted in the engine, this means that removing a node from the tree will not delete it (remove_child). To better manage nodes, create a pool of objects and re-use the node object, you can also explicitly destroy/free the node from memory by calling "queue_free()" in the "_exit_tree()" which is called when the node is removed from the tree.

-------Operands-------
    Operators: Are symbols used to manipulate and check operand values, they can be numerical, text, and Boolean values.
    10 + 20
    x - 1
    1 + 2
    x - y.

    Assignment Operators: Assign a value to it's left operand based on the value of it's right operand.
    x = x + y, x += y (Additional Assignment)
    x = x - y, x -+ y (Subtraction Assignment)
    x = x * y, x *= y (Multiplication Assignment)
    x = x / y, x /= y (Division Assignment)
    x = x % y, x %= y (Remainder Assignment)

    Comparison Operators: Compares it's operands and returns a logical value based on whether the comparison is true.
    x == 1 (are equal)
    x != 1 (are not equal)
    x > 1 (left is greater than right)
    x < 1 (right is greater than left)
    x >= 1 (left is greater than right, or equal)
    x <= 1 (right is greater than left, or equal)

    Logical Operators:
    1. &&/AND: Will first check to see if the left operand can be converted to false. If it can, the value false will be returned. Otherwise, it will check to see if the second operand can be converted to false. If the second operand can be converted to false, it will return false. Otherwise, it will return true.
    var x = true
    x && 1 #returns true

    2. ||/OR: Will return true as long as any of the operands can be converted to true, starting from the left most operand.
    var x = false
    x || 1 #returns true

    3. !/NOT: Will return false if it's operand can be converted to true, otherwise, it will return true.
    var x = false
    !x #returns true

-------Constants-------
    A Constant is a value that never changes, each of them need a unique name or an error will be thrown. They're declared with:
    const ValueNeverChanges = 1
    You may also add a data type explicitly by adding a colon followed by the data type. Not necessary as the data type is inferred with the assigned value.
    const ValueNeverChanges: int = 1
    Constant Declaration Format:
    const ValueName ( : Data Type) = Literal/Data Object
    Constants are ready only, therefore modifications are not permitted after declaration and assignment.
    Not permitted:
    const ValueNeverChanges = 1
    ValueNeverChanges = 100 #will throw an error
    Pro's of Using Constant: Readable code, easier Debugging, save time when editing code.
    You can use literals instead of constants. To the compiler it doesn't make a difference how values are given, however, for the sake of readable code and easier debugging you should use constants.
    var playerSpeed = 5
    var laserSpeed = 5
    var boomerangSpeed = 5
    var enemySpeed = 5
    #5 is the main speed for game items, if you needed to change all the speed, you'd have to manualy change each single one, to avoid this, you can make a constant variable presenting the default speed.
    const Speed = 5
    var playerSpeed = Speed
    var laserSpeed = Speed
    var boomerangSpeed = Speed
    var enemySpeed = Speed
    #Another example of when to use variables and constants
    const:
    const playerName = "John"
